/* book Worked example: Q: A constant-temperature hot-wire anemometer follows E² = A + B√u with A = 4 V² and B = 2 V²/(m/s)0.5. (a) Find E at u = 9 m/s. (b) Find u when E = 3.46 V. (c) A resistive hygrometer reads 1 MΩ at 30 % RH and 100 kΩ at 60 % RH. Using a log-linear law, find R at 45 % RH.
   1. (a) E² = 4 + 2 × √9 = 4 + 2 × 3 = 10 V². E = √10 = 3.16 V.
   2. (b) E² = 3.46² = 11.97 V². Then (11.97 − 4)/2 = 3.986 = √u, so u = 3.986² = 15.9 m/s.
   3. (c) From 30 % to 60 % RH the resistance falls one decade, 1 MΩ to 100 kΩ, so log R falls 1/30 decade per %RH. At 45 % RH, 15 % above 30 %, R = 1 MΩ × 10−15/30 = 1 MΩ/√10 = 316.2 kΩ.
   4. Note in (a) and (b): u rose 1.77 times (9 to 15.9 m/s) while E rose only 9.5 % (3.16 to 3.46 V); the square-root law compresses high speeds, so resolution is poorest there.
   Answer: E = 3.16 V at 9 m/s; u = 15.9 m/s at 3.46 V; R = 316.2 kΩ at 45 % RH. */
