/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A thermocouple is a closed loop of two dissimilar metals with two junctions.
   - The modern picture: in each wire, carriers at the hot end diffuse toward the cold end, so every wire across a temperature gradient develops an emf along it.
   - Two heat effects go with it.
   - Only the difference of the two wires’ emfs survives, so both junctions at the same temperature give exactly 0 mV, whatever that temperature is.
   - E is the Seebeck emf in mV; S is the Seebeck coefficient of the pair in µV/°C; Th and Tc are the hot and cold junction temperatures in °C.
   - Three laws turn a pair of junctions into an instrument.
   - E(Th, Tc) is the meter reading in mV; E(Tc, 0 °C) is the table value at the cold-junction temperature; E(Th, 0 °C) is the table value you look up to get Th.
   - Read backwards: the meter's millivolts are a temperature difference, never an absolute temperature.
   - Types are fixed by alloy pair.
   - The instrument must supply E(Tc, 0 °C) itself.
*/
